ally seems to be  the b117 variant any thoughts as to why  b117 is having such a dramatic effect  in michigan but we're not seeing it in  indiana  we're not seeing it in illinois or even  really in wisconsin  it's been kind of an interesting  phenomenon do we think that that is  more of a localized patient or you know  more of a localized population issue  or do you think that there's something  special about that variant  uh it's a great question great  observation nate  when i think about the answer i just  look to the data in england because  it was identified  early on in southeast england but when  they finally looked at the data across  the regions they realized it was moving  pretty rapidly across all of england  over the period of like three or four  weeks so  i think you're correct yeah michigan is  is a hot spot right now  but i think next week the week after  we're gonna find it's in  illinois it's in indiana it's in  wisconsin  you know it's already here in  massachusetts uh it is more infectious  you know i've seen different numbers of  infectivity 1.5 or so more than the  wuhan strain  so we really have to you know keep a  close eye on the recommendations by the  cdc for for  masking and you know all of the the good  things we've been doing to keep this  pandemic under control  thank you so much gentlemen now dr  larabar you brought up  regulations and some of the barriers  someone just did a follow-up question  and said how do you plan or maybe all  labs plan to deal with those kinds of  regulatory barriers  so i think number one we've already seen  a shift and i mean the fda really  you know they've gotten a lot of  criticism but they've actually responded  very very nicely they're they're very  very quickly reviewing assays getting  them onto the market  um they have really moved to the idea of  trying to get things sped through  the approval process as well i think one  of the smartest things the fda did  very early on was to say okay if you're  using a modification of the cdc assay  you can fall under the cdc's eua  approval  and you can go with it it's  substantially equivalent um  i yeah but i think at the end of the day  how do we prepare this for the next  pandemic  i think it's really going to come down  to our professional societies whether it  be  the college of american pathologists  whether it be the uh  you know whether it be ascp or the  american society for clinical pathology  aacc amp asm  you know any idsa any of the our  societies or all of our societies  really coming to the fda and saying this  is the framework that we need to build  to respond to the next pandemic  and that's going to have to be a very  collaborative effort  and it's going to have to be done also  in collaboration with the who as well  so i think it's going to be you know a  multi-year conversation in terms of how  do we change that regulatory framework  so that it can be responsive we've  certainly also  seen um some interesting information  coming out of the  inspector general inspector general's  office of the fda  really questioning whether the fda has  the ability to regulate lab developed  tests as we continue that conversation a  bit further  um it will be interesting to hear  you know where we ultimately land on  laboratory developed tests and  depending on where that answer  ultimately comes that may be a very  viable option for labs too going forward  thank you so much and this quest next  question i think is on the minds of  everyone how  quickly is this virus mutating  i think it's always mutating and the  question is  is are there any mutations that will end  up being of high consequence  and um so it remains to be determined  um the just a little more background on  on this virus is that many of these  mutations have come  from you know compromised patients  unfortunately because it seems to  reproduce  effectively in immunocompromised  patients who have extended periods of  activity  so again the more we can do to keep the  virus out of the general population  especially immunocompromised populations  um  the crook will be able to get it in  control nate do you have anything to add  to that  yeah i i would just say i mean if we  look at some of the data on it we're  seeing  about seven new variants the data i  recently saw was about seven new  variants a week  that certainly isn't the frequency of  mutation that's variants that ultimately  survive because we have to remember the  vast majority of these variants that  emerge  ultimately are not favorable variants to  the virus and  ultimately that lineage ultimately dies  off so  it's certainly not as bad as it could be  but it's certainly  you know we are seeing a reasonable  degree of mutation that's occurring  thank you so much we have time for one  more question gentlemen  and we'll kind of continue our  discussion about regulations you clearly  show the importance of  lods what changes or recommendations  would you make to the fda or the euas  or other regulatory bodies to ensure  assay performance  meets the requirements for lods and  patient care  so there's two really important things  that are happening  in this particular case susie first of  all we've seen  the first of we've seen the first couple  of  510k approved tests um bio fire for  example went through and got  510k on their respiratory panel  via the de novo 510k submission route so  we're beginning to see the fda  establish a standard for limited  detection  for these tests and i think  you know these first or these early  adopters or the early movers to go  through the 510k process are really the  pioneers and we're going to be moving  we're going to see a lot of additional  assays move through that traditional fda  review route so i think that will be an  important  gating step we've also seen again as  we've seen more contemporary assays move  through the approval process  there is a bit more standardization in  terms of what would be accepted as the  reference standard  how sensitive that test needs to be and  so we see  ultimately a a bit of a more rigorous  comparison that's occurring  again the key issue with this though is  balancing the speed at which we get  assays onto the market really responding  to that issue of  how do we make sure that we develop and  have adequate capacity that's  present on the market with ensuring that  we have good  accurate and reliable assays that are on  market  so i think it will be really important  and really key  for manufacturers moving forward to make  sure that their assays  are designed to be sensitive that they  will identify  the different variants that emerge and  that the assays are  you know well suited for uh for use in  the field if you will  dr garland dr luttabor i want to thank  you gentlemen for your time today and  for your  important research and your presentation  and before we go  i also want to thank our audience for  joining us today  and for their interesting questions i'd  also like to thank our sponsor  lgc sarahcare for sponsoring today's  webinar  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by our speakers via  the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  and this webcast  can be viewed on demand for two years  until  april of 2023 laboratory alert you via  email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share  that email with colleagues who may have  missed today's live event  until next time stay safe and stay  healthy everyone take care bye
 

